WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.960
Monty Python-inspired absurdity,&nbsp;
Scientology controversies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.460
and post-COVID satire - Comedy Central's South&nbsp;
Park has never shied away from controversy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.360
Is it the "Holy Grail" of animated&nbsp;
sitcoms or just plain sacrilegious?

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.400
"South Park" creators Matt Stone and Trey Parker&nbsp;
owe a great deal of credit to Monty Python,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.020
the British sketch comedy collective that rose&nbsp;
to prominence in the 1960s and '70s with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:27.180
unique brand of absurd, satirical humor. The group&nbsp;
consisted of comedy icons including John Cleese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.260
Graham Chapman, and Eric Idle, whose many&nbsp;
projects heavily influenced Parker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.560
Stone throughout the early development&nbsp;
process of "South Park." Particularly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.680
the show's iconic art style was&nbsp;
influenced by the Terry Gilliam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.700
animations that introduced their BBC sketch&nbsp;
series, "Monty Python's Flying Circus."

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.180
Over the years, Stone and Parker have&nbsp;
had the opportunity to pay tribute to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.540
their comedy heroes. In celebration&nbsp;
of the troupe's 50th anniversary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.200
they contributed a "South Park" recreation&nbsp;
of the iconic "dead parrot" sketch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.260
with Cartman attempting to return&nbsp;
a dead Kenny to the "friend store."

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.720
"I'll tell you what's wrong&nbsp;
with him, my lad, he's dead!"&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.600
"No no no, he's uh, he's resting."

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.220
The troupe's reunion show at the O2 in&nbsp;
London in 2014 was apparently sparked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.260
by the "South Park" duo, although Idle jokingly&nbsp;
countered this claim in a Reddit AMA by saying —

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.500
"They are arrogant little twerps trying&nbsp;
to get cheap publicity off our backs."

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.580
Idle's comment was likely in jest, given&nbsp;
that the comedian provided the voice of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.020
Dr. Vosknocker in "South Park: Bigger,&nbsp;
Longer, &amp; Uncut." The "South Park" duo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.080
also had a relationship with Python&nbsp;
member and film director Terry Gilliam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.340
who once advised the two to not overextend&nbsp;
themselves across several projects. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.420
advice was promptly ignored, as the pair produced&nbsp;
"South Park" alongside several feature films.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.080
"Oh, it's you! Alright buddy,&nbsp;
you stop that right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.820
This is our show! Whaaaa…"
"Ay, I'm not fat, I'm big-boned!"

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.780
The success of "South Park" has been the&nbsp;
subject of discussion in the entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.440
industry ever since it first premiered.&nbsp;
It's not incredibly surprising that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.800
show took off given its success as a&nbsp;
viral video in the Internet's infancy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.260
but the first episode's debut on Comedy Central&nbsp;
in 1997 was no accident either. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.200
test audiences weren't responsive&nbsp;
to the series' first episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.140
which made the network hesitate in&nbsp;
giving the boys a further chance.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.760
Season 1 was nevertheless the subject of a&nbsp;
fierce marketing campaign by Comedy Central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.780
as a last-ditch effort to draw attention&nbsp;
to it. "South Park" even labeled itself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.320
the reason for parents to install a V-chip in&nbsp;
their television to monitor their children's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.760
viewing habits. Even before the first episode&nbsp;
premiered, Comedy Central had already raked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.780
in a fortune in T-shirt sales, proving that&nbsp;
the series was to be an inevitable success.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.740
"South Park" is also responsible for&nbsp;
changing public opinion of Comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.280
Central as a network entirely. It's had&nbsp;
as much of an influence on modern-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.000
TV as shows like "The Simpsons," but&nbsp;
the appeal of "South Park" to a newer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.480
generation made it a surefire success&nbsp;
for the network and the show's creators.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.680
Outside the world of Internet memes&nbsp;
and pop culture, "South Park" has even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.400
influenced the world of law. The show's&nbsp;
frequent mocking of celebrities, songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.360
and moments have drawn plenty of discussion&nbsp;
over fair use and parody law, like the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.560
when Butters performed a viral song originally&nbsp;
made by the Internet personality Samwell. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.000
resulting lawsuit was eventually dismissed, but&nbsp;
it set a precedent concerning fair use laws.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.620
"South Park" has also pervaded the lexicon of not&nbsp;
just American culture, but of the American court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.480
system as well. This influence started in the&nbsp;
Season 2 episode "Chef Aid," which revolves around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.340
Chef suing Alanis Morissette for plagiarizing&nbsp;
him. Hired by Morissette's record label, celebrity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.140
lawyer Johnnie Cochran — who notably defended&nbsp;
O.J. Simpson during his widely publicized murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.640
trial — successfully argues for his client by&nbsp;
using what's now known as the "Chewbacca defense."

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.520
"Why would a wookiee, an eight foot tall wookiee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.140
want to live on Endor with a bunch of two&nbsp;
foot tall Ewoks? That does not make sense."

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.200
Although Chewbacca himself isn't&nbsp;
often mentioned in lawsuits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.400
the term "Chewbacca defense" now blankets any&nbsp;
argument made by the prosecution or defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.020
that is meant to deliberately confuse the&nbsp;
jury. As recently as 2022, the "South Park"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:47.340
episode was referenced during a federal tax&nbsp;
fraud case in Jacksonville, Florida. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.660
the argument's efficacy when not employed by&nbsp;
Cochran in "South Park" hasn't had much success.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.820
Even though "South Park" had only been airing&nbsp;
on Comedy Central for two seasons in 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.560
it had already become a pop culture phenomenon.&nbsp;
Naturally, this warranted a transition to the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.860
screen, with Parker and Stone co-producing&nbsp;
the first "South Park" feature film:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.480
"South Park: Bigger, Longer &amp; Uncut." Parker&nbsp;
assumed directorial duties for the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.600
which he co-wrote with Stone and&nbsp;
veteran "South Park" writer Pam Brady.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:16.860
The film centers on the typical "South Park"&nbsp;
kids sneaking into an R-rated screening of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.280
Terrance and Phillip. The foul language&nbsp;
in the movie gets them to start swearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.600
resulting in a nationwide panic that causes&nbsp;
the United States to declare war on Canada.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.120
"Just remember what the MPAA says:&nbsp;
horrific, deplorable violence is okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.404
as long as people don't say any naughty words!"

00:04:33.404 --> 00:04:35.040
If that wasn't wild enough on its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.220
the whole thing is a musical featuring&nbsp;
some delightfully vulgar songs.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.280
"Bigger, Longer &amp; Uncut" was&nbsp;
well received — Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.770
Post critic Rita Kempley wrote in her review —

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.080
"The outrageously profane "South Park: Bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.640
Longer &amp; Uncut" takes wicked whacks at targets&nbsp;
ranging from Saddam Hussein to the notion that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.120
the entertainment industry is chiefly to&nbsp;
blame for the pollution of kids' culture."

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.440
Despite the film's lampooning of the entertainment&nbsp;
industry and censorship, "South Park: Bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:02.700
Longer &amp; Uncut" was a success in its own&nbsp;
right. It was the highest-grossing R-rated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:07.320
movie ever for a time, and the musical number&nbsp;
"Blame Canada" even garnered an Academy Award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.120
nomination for Best Original Song. However, even&nbsp;
the show's Oscar appearance garnered controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.900
with ABC censors ironically requesting&nbsp;
the performance of the song be altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.700
for airing. Meanwhile, Parker and Stone showed&nbsp;
up to the prestigious ceremony after taking LSD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.480
wearing dresses previously worn by&nbsp;
Jennifer Lopez and Gwyneth Paltrow.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:29.160
While Parker and Stone are best known as the&nbsp;
minds behind "South Park" for over 20 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:33.660
they've also made waves in the world of musical&nbsp;
theater. Along with "Frozen" songwriter Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.920
Lopez, they co-created the smash hit Broadway&nbsp;
musical "The Book of Mormon." The nine-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.140
Tony-winning show premiered in 2011, starring&nbsp;
Andrew Rannells and Josh Gad as two Mormon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.860
missionaries who are sent to a poverty-stricken&nbsp;
Ugandan village to spread their faith.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.240
Parker and Stone met Lopez after seeing his&nbsp;
musical "Avenue Q" during the production of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:55.440
film "Team America: World Police." Together,&nbsp;
the trio bonded over their shared interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.700
in musical theater and Mormonism — Parker and&nbsp;
Stone grew up in a Mormon community in Colorado,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.300
hence their fascination. However, this&nbsp;
interest didn't start with "The Book of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:07.080
Mormon," as the duo had previously taken&nbsp;
shots at the religion in earlier projects.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:11.280
In addition to their 1997 movie,&nbsp;
"Orgazmo," which features Mormonism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.880
the duo parodied the religion in&nbsp;
Season 7 of "South Park." The episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.960
"All About Mormons," follows a Utah family&nbsp;
moving to South Park and features a sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.380
explaining how Joseph Smith founded&nbsp;
the religion. Even Mormons themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.790
have appreciated the jokes, with blogger&nbsp;
Steve Evans writing for By Common Consent —

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:30.780
"South Park argues that the social aspects of&nbsp;
our faith may be sufficient unto themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.740
regardless of the [efficacy] of our ordinances&nbsp;
or the historicity of our scriptures."

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.100
"And maybe Joseph Smith did make&nbsp;
it all up. But I have a great life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.220
and a great family, and I have the&nbsp;
Book of Mormon to thank for that!"

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.900
"South Park" has lampooned many celebrities&nbsp;
over the course of its 26 seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.980
garnering more than enough bad blood between&nbsp;
the show's creators and famous figures. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.860
one of the series' most notable&nbsp;
controversies came during their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.700
ninth season, when they parodied the Church&nbsp;
of Scientology during the episode "Trapped in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:01.020
the Closet." It famously featured a sequence&nbsp;
depicting the Scientologist origin myth, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.100
well as the implication that several celebrity&nbsp;
Scientologists are actually closeted themselves.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.780
One person the "South Park" duo probably&nbsp;
didn't anticipate angering was Isaac Hayes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.800
the singer-songwriter who played the&nbsp;
iconic role of Chef. Also a member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.460
the Church of Scientology, Hayes announced&nbsp;
in a statement prior to Season 10 that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.580
would be departing the show for their&nbsp;
disrespect of the religion. The matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.960
became the subject of controversy for years&nbsp;
to come, with Matt Stone telling The AP —

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.560
"We never heard a peep out of Isaac in&nbsp;
any way until we did Scientology. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.020
wants a different standard for&nbsp;
religions other than his own."

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:35.280
The circumstances of Hayes' departure have&nbsp;
since been contradicted, with some reports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.360
saying that Hayes was also dissatisfied with&nbsp;
pay or had suffered a stroke. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.260
this meant the end of Chef's time on "South&nbsp;
Park," with the character being killed off in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.860
the Season 10 premiere after being struck&nbsp;
by lightning and mauled by wild animals.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:51.480
The speed at which the "South Park" production&nbsp;
team moves has resulted in some pretty close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:55.200
calls when it comes to episodes airing. One&nbsp;
of the most famous examples of this came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.500
during the show's 12th season, when the&nbsp;
team decided to do an episode that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.980
air the day after the 2008 presidential&nbsp;
election without knowing who would win.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:06.360
The episode's production was entirely reliant&nbsp;
on Trey Parker's hope that Barack Obama would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.960
defeat John McCain. The "South Park"&nbsp;
co-creator told The Los Angeles Times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.370
that they considered making two versions of the&nbsp;
episode, but it wasn't feasible. He elaborated —

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.140
"Well, we’re just going to make the Obama version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.500
and if McCain somehow wins, we’re&nbsp;
basically just totally screwed."

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.980
"Wooooo! Obama!"
"Get out of here!"

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.800
Thankfully for them, Obama did&nbsp;
inevitably win the election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:30.180
allowing the team to proceed with the episode&nbsp;
as is. However, they still incorporated some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.540
of his victory speech into the episode by&nbsp;
the morning of the day the episode aired.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.900
It was also decided by Parker and the other&nbsp;
members of the "South Park" team that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:41.640
episode's plot would be removed from bipartisan&nbsp;
politics. Instead, Obama and McCain would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.420
attempting to pull off a jewel heist, while the&nbsp;
political alignment of each recurring character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.320
was decided arbitrarily during production.&nbsp;
Thankfully, no one had to worry for long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:53.760
about messing up the election prediction, but&nbsp;
it made for an incredibly lucky call by Parker.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:58.200
"South Park" has been no stranger to the world&nbsp;
of video games, receiving its first playable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.420
adaptation on the Nintendo 64 and Playstation in&nbsp;
1998. Other "South Park" video games have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.440
released over the years, but they're essentially&nbsp;
existing games with a new coat of paint. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.280
why it eventually became a priority for Parker&nbsp;
and Stone to faithfully translate the "South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.000
Park" experience into a video game. Their&nbsp;
dream finally came true with "South Park:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.820
The Stick of Truth," which was released for&nbsp;
PlayStation 3, Xbox 360, and PC in 2014.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.540
While previous "South Park" video games had&nbsp;
experimented with genres like first-person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.560
shooters and racing, "The Stick of Truth" is a&nbsp;
role-playing game in which players control the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.240
new kid during a town-wide LARPing session. Though&nbsp;
the game was developed by Obsidian Entertainment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:37.380
Parker and Stone were heavily involved in the&nbsp;
game's design. They took heavy inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:41.880
from Nintendo's "Earthbound," another&nbsp;
popular RPG that Parker particularly loved.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.900
The game's development process was also&nbsp;
subject to similar difficulties as the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:50.640
itself. Censors in different countries resulted&nbsp;
in humorous workarounds by the development team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.020
including in one scene where Randy Marsh&nbsp;
gets probed by aliens. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:58.860
like the "South Park" TV show, "The Stick of&nbsp;
Truth" was an incredible success critically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:03.390
and commercially. A sequel released in&nbsp;
2017, "South Park: The Fractured but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:07.620
Whole," trades the medieval fantasy roleplay of&nbsp;
"The Stick of Truth" for superhero alter-egos.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.940
Like the rest of the entertainment industry,&nbsp;
"South Park" was affected by the COVID-19&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.660
pandemic. Thanks to the production team's&nbsp;
quick turnaround time on creating episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:20.160
though, they were able to release "The Pandemic&nbsp;
Special" in September of 2020 as part of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.200
24th season. This hour-long special focuses&nbsp;
on the town's reaction to COVID-19, as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:30.000
Randy Marsh's role in spreading it. It was then&nbsp;
followed up in March 2021 with the "South ParQ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.920
Vaccination Special," continuing the storyline&nbsp;
by addressing vaccines and QAnon conspiracies.

00:10:35.203 --> 00:10:38.400
"You guys have a right to say&nbsp;
and believe whatever you want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.239
okay? But what you believe is really stupid."

00:10:41.239 --> 00:10:45.780
Later in 2021, "South Park" extended its deal&nbsp;
with Comedy Central to produce the show for up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:49.920
to 30 seasons, including two films that were&nbsp;
planned to be released that year. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.660
of them was "South Park: Post COVID," a one-hour&nbsp;
special followed up by a second hour, "South Park:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:59.100
Post COVID: The Return of COVID." Unlike "The&nbsp;
Pandemic Special," the "Post COVID" specials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:03.060
did the impossible and took a look at the lives of&nbsp;
the show's main cast forty years into the future.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:07.020
The specials find Stan returning to&nbsp;
South Park after Kenny has passed away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:11.820
resulting in the trio of Stan, Kyle, and&nbsp;
Cartman — who's now a rabbi — reuniting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:16.380
finish research on a COVID-19 cure. Eventually,&nbsp;
they discover that Kenny discovered time travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:20.520
so Stan and Kyle try using it to prevent&nbsp;
the pandemic. They're unable to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:24.420
but they still manage to improve the lives&nbsp;
of everyone they know… except for Cartman.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:30.480
In 2022, "South Park" had its 25th anniversary.&nbsp;
That was cause for celebration for Parker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:34.440
Stone, who took the festivities to their home&nbsp;
state of Colorado and put on an extravagant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:38.940
concert at the iconic Red Rocks Amphitheater&nbsp;
in Morrison. The setlist contained many musical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:42.960
hits from "South Park" history, including the&nbsp;
Oscar-nominated "Blame Canada" from "South Park:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:47.100
Bigger, Longer &amp; Uncut" and the anime-inspired&nbsp;
ninja anthem "Let's Fighting Love."

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.900
The concert also featured special guests,&nbsp;
with bands Ween and Primus supporting Stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:54.900
and Parker throughout the concert.&nbsp;
However, the biggest surprises were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:59.100
Geddy Lee and Alex Lifeson from Rush, who&nbsp;
surprised not only the audience, but Stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:03.720
too. Stone joined the duo on drums to perform&nbsp;
the beloved Rush song "Closer to the Heart."

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:07.800
The once-in-a-lifetime concert was performed&nbsp;
across two nights, and later made available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:12.360
to watch on Paramount+ and YouTube. It's&nbsp;
a must-watch for any "South Park" fan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.960
as they'll get to see the geniuses behind the&nbsp;
show celebrate having one of the most successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:21.180
animated shows of all time for 25 years. Here's&nbsp;
to many more anniversaries on the horizon.

